examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $v\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and $v\\ne 0,$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ is not a bijection and the matrix $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186492858 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{dim}(\\text{span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=1$ this means that $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$ According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the invertible matrix theorem the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0$ is equivalent to $Tx=0.$ Since $T$ is not invertible, the equation has non-trivial solutions.\n\n- Statement III is false. From statement I, $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\""] [17592186492918 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $v$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$, we have that $T(v)=0.$ Therefore $v\\in \\text{Ker}(T),$ thus according to the invertible matrix theorem $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since there is not $u\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(u)=[1,0,0]^{T},$ we have that $T$ is not  a bijection (an invertible transformation). According to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [17592186500304 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $3\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{x}2(-t+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={8t-{t}^{2}|}_{3}^{x} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+8x-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<3 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-15, & 3\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
